3.	Shortly prior -to, or at the sale on March 3, of a portion of the lands of the Saddle Lake Indian reserve, Allberta, did the Indian agent at the said reserve retoeive instructions that intending purchasers securing first choices would 'be entitled to purchase as much land as they desired?
2.	If so, were these the original instructions of the government to the said agent, and if not the original instructions, on whose advice, request or suggestion and on what ground was the change in such original instructions made?
3.	Were the names of the persons who actually drew and purchased portions of the said land only those set-out below; and did each purchase the amount of land set out below opposite his name?
J. Gagnon, 8 quarter sections;
J. Conrnack, 3 quarter sections;
Jacobs, 3 quarter sections;
H. Fontaine, 19 quarter sections;
V. Tessier, 5 quarter sections;
A. Poitras, 7 quarter sections;
P, Charron, 2 quarter sections.
1 Purchaser (name unknown), 1 quarter section.
4.	If the above names and amounts of land were not those of the persons who actually purchased on the said 3rd or 4th of March, what were the names of acd amounts purchased by such original purchasers respectively?
5.	Were one hundred and nineteen (119) tickets actually drawn by applicants to purchase?
6.	Were initial payments on their said purchases made by any of the said purchasers to the agent on the said 3rd or 4th of Mandh or shortly thereafter?
7.	Were other persons who had not themselves drawn tickets entitling them to purchase, subsequently summoned to the house of the said agent and desired or allowed themselves to become direct purchasers of lands or portions of such lands?
8.	Does the list of purchasers given by the Minister *of the Interior in answer to question (8) of the ques-

tions put to him on April 15th (Hansard 27th May, p. 3902), Include as well such subsequent purchasers as th" said original purchasers? If so, which are they?

I quite agree with my hon. friend from Argenteuil (Sir George Perley)-or rather the acting minister of many departments-that no member has a right to speak disrespectfully in this House of His Excellency the Governor General or of the crown. But from what I have heard of the remarks
of the hon. member for Winnipeg North Centre (Mr. Woodsworth) I fail to see that he has uttered one word of such a character. Let me point out however that for any act of the crown the Prime Minister of 5 p.m. the day must take responsibility;
and when an hon. member of this House accepted the invitation to form a cabinet he assumed responsibility for His Excellency's declining to grant a dissolution to the then Prime Minister. Any discussion that takes place on the subject, any reference that is made to the matter, has relation to the conduct of the Prime Minister of Canada who in this instance, I claim, assumed responsibility for what I consider a breach of the constitution.

I wish to say simply
this-that the hon. members who in the past have co-operated wdth the government, have frequently resented the use of that word as applied to that co-operation. They have refuted it by stating plainly on more than one occasion the basis and the procedure of the co-operation between the two groups;
and when we see, on occasions of this kind, a considerable section of those who have been co-operating with the late government voting so directly in contradiction to their known principles-principles which I admit they hold now just as strongly as they did before the vote-
